AB  - This study, published in the American Journal of Sociology, examines how criminal co-offending creates pathways between neighborhoods, influencing the spatial distribution of crime. By analyzing administrative records and survey data from Chicago, the authors demonstrate that co-offending acts form a "network of neighborhoods" that facilitates crime diffusion over time and space. Their findings show that these networks are stable, shaped by structural characteristics and social processes, and are better predictors of crime distribution than traditional spatial models.
